content.text:
Current CSS:

```css
:host {
  min-height: 0;
  flex: 1;
}
```

- `:host`: targets `<tc-review-list>` itself.
- `min-height: 0`: allows it to shrink inside a flex container instead of overflowing.
- `flex: 1`: fills available space in its parent.

```css
.wrapper {
  padding: 20px;
  height: 100%;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
```

- `padding: 20px`: inner spacing.
- `height: 100%`: fills the component’s height.
- `display: flex`: enables flex layout.
- `flex-direction: column`: arranges content vertically.
- `box-sizing: border-box`: includes padding within the declared height.

```css
.scrollable-area {
  overflow: auto;
}
```

- Adds scrollbars only when the review lists exceed available space.

```css
.review-entry {
  display: -webkit-box;
  white-space: normal;
  -webkit-box-orient: vertical;
  -webkit-line-clamp: 2;
}
```

- `display: -webkit-box`: enables the legacy browser layout required by `-webkit-line-clamp`.
- `white-space: normal`: permits wrapping. This overrides Material’s single-line behavior.
- `-webkit-box-orient: vertical`: makes the box arrange text vertically into lines.
- `-webkit-line-clamp: 2`: displays the beginning of the text, limits it to two lines, and adds `…`.

```css
.close-button {
  margin-top: auto;
  align-self: start;
}
```

- `margin-top: auto`: pushes the button to the bottom.
- `align-self: start`: left-aligns it instead of stretching.

You do not currently need `overflow: hidden` on `.review-entry` because Angular Material’s list item already clips overflowing content, while `-webkit-line-clamp` calculates the two-line truncation and ellipsis.

That is context-dependent. Adding `overflow: hidden` would make `.review-entry` self-contained and safer if Material’s styles change, but it currently produces no visible difference.
